<a href="#section-send-architecture" id="section-send-architecture" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Inside a mail send</h2>
<section>
  <p class="wide">These are the pieces behind the configuration on this page. The executor controls waiting work, the pool manages reusable
    connections, and the transport talks SMTP. Outcome reporting follows each email across those layers; it is separate from the work of sending it.</p>
  {{> components/mail-send-architecture}}
  <p class="wide">This is a responsibility map, not a lock-order diagram. Direct sends, shared batches, custom mailers, and caller-owned Sessions
    have different paths. Use the sections below for their execution and ownership rules.</p>
</section>

<a href="#section-send-execution" id="section-send-execution" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Choose how sends execute</h2>
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <p><code>sendMailSync(email)</code> sends on the calling thread and throws failures directly.
      <code>sendMailAndGetReceiptSync(email)</code> does the same and returns a submission receipt.
      Use the Async variants when the caller should continue while SMTP work runs.</p>
    <p>Async sends return <code>MailSend&lt;T&gt;</code>, where T is Void or MailSubmissionReceipt.
      The default-mode and boolean send methods, including simple batches, return the same handle.
      With synchronous mode, they throw failures directly and return an already completed handle on success.</p>
    <p><code>getCompletion()</code> returns a detached CompletableFuture for the actual result. Each call creates a fresh view.
      Completing, cancelling, or applying <code>orTimeout(...)</code> to that view cannot change the send.
      Use <code>requestCancellation()</code> when you intend to stop the underlying work.</p>
    <p>Ordinary email preparation and validation stay on the caller thread, even for async sends. Their failures go onto the returned completion;
      a null email remains an immediate argument error. Simple batches retain lazy iteration on their execution thread.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="side"><pre><code class="language-java">MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send =
    mailer.sendMailAndGetReceiptAsync(email);

send.getCompletion().whenComplete((receipt, failure) -&gt; {
    if (failure == null) {
        recordSubmission(receipt);
    } else {
        recordFailure(failure);
    }
});

// Or wait, without losing the ability to request cancellation:
MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = send.getCompletion().join();</code></pre></div>
</section>
  <a href="#section-async-queue" id="section-async-queue" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Bound asynchronous work and handle a full queue{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        A background job can produce emails faster than the SMTP server accepts them. Limiting workers alone leaves the waiting queue free to grow.
        Set an async queue capacity when you want the application to notice overload and decide whether to pause the producer, retain work in its own
        durable queue, or skip a notification that is no longer useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        The default capacity is <code>-1</code> (unbounded). A positive capacity limits waiting tasks; <code>0</code> permits only immediate handoff
        to a worker. This is one queue per Mailer, not per SMTP connection or cluster. The SMTP work still runs on a worker, never as a full-queue fallback on the caller.
      </p>
      <p>
        The example rejects immediately once workers and waiting slots are occupied. Rejection completes the returned future exceptionally with
        <code>MailSendRejectedException</code>. An ordinary email attempt also reaches the configured observer once on the submitting thread, with no
        submission receipt and no started timestamp. No transport or proxy is acquired for the rejected task.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587, "user", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withThreadPoolSize(4) // with batch-module; otherwise one worker
    .withAsyncQueueCapacity(100)
    .withAsyncQueueOverflowPolicy(AsyncQueueOverflowPolicy.REJECT)
    .buildMailer();

mailer.sendMailAsync(email).getCompletion().whenComplete((ignored, failure) -&gt; {
    Throwable cause = failure instanceof CompletionException ? failure.getCause() : failure;
    if (cause instanceof MailSendRejectedException) {
        MailSendRejectedException rejected = (MailSendRejectedException) cause;
        logger.warn("Mail was not scheduled: {}", rejected.getReason());
    } else if (cause != null) {
        logger.warn("Mail send failed", cause);
    }
});</code></pre>
      <p>
        Rejection means this attempt was not scheduled. Other send failures can represent an accepted or ambiguous SMTP transaction:
        inspect the <a href="features.html#section-submission-receipts">submission receipt</a> before deciding to retry.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <h4>Let a background producer wait briefly</h4>
      <p>
        For a producer that can slow down, <code>WAIT_FOR_CAPACITY</code> waits on the submitting thread until a queue slot opens or the positive
        admission timeout expires. The default timeout is 1000ms. Avoid this policy on request or event-loop threads that must return promptly.
        An observer submitting another async send can also wait; with a full one-worker queue, only the timeout may let that worker continue.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>CAPACITY_WAIT_TIMED_OUT</code>, <code>CAPACITY_WAIT_INTERRUPTED</code>, and <code>EXECUTOR_SHUT_DOWN</code> explain unsuccessful waits;
        <code>QUEUE_FULL</code> is immediate rejection. An interrupted admission wait preserves the calling thread's interrupt flag.
        The wait timeout is not a send deadline and does not cancel SMTP.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withAsyncQueueCapacity(100)
    .withAsyncQueueOverflowPolicy(AsyncQueueOverflowPolicy.WAIT_FOR_CAPACITY)
    .withAsyncQueueWaitTimeoutMillis(250);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <h4>Know what the bound counts</h4>
      <p>
        Worker count, waiting queue capacity, and connection-pool size are independent. A worker waiting for a pool lease still counts as active.
        Queued tasks are FIFO; concurrent execution and completion need not be. Email preparation remains on the caller before queue admission,
        so the bound does not limit memory used by simultaneous callers preparing emails.
      </p>
      <p>
        An asynchronous simple batch takes one slot and opens its iterable only when execution starts. Rejected batches do not touch the iterator or
        report outcomes for its emails. Async connection tests share the queue; synchronous and open-connection operations do not use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        A caller-owned executor defines its own queue, policy, and shutdown. Combining one with non-default built-in queue settings fails when building
        the Mailer; use <code>resetAsyncQueue()</code> before <code>withExecutorService(...)</code> to discard them explicitly.
        The standalone <code>BatchTransportExecutor</code> has a separate executor and is not configured by these settings.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use <a href="debugging.html#section-async-queue">queue snapshots</a> for counts and rejection reasons, and
        <a href="#section-mail-send-observer">observer timestamps</a> for individual queue delay. For lifecycle behavior, see
        <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">graceful shutdown</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


<a href="#section-send-deadlines" id="section-send-deadlines" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Deadlines and cancellation</h2>
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <p>A notification may stop being useful while it waits: a campaign was paused, a job was cancelled, or a newer status update replaced it.
      Keep its send handle if you want to request cancellation. For a whole-operation time budget, configure a Mailer deadline.</p>
    <p><code>requestCancellation()</code> is a void, idempotent request, not an acknowledgement. Always inspect the eventual completion.
      A send can still succeed if SMTP acceptance was already observed. If the final reply was lost after the terminating DATA command
      or BDAT LAST could have reached the server, the receipt remains UNKNOWN with DUPLICATE_RISK. Cancellation does not recall accepted mail.</p>
    <p><code>MailSendCancelledException</code> and <code>MailSendTimeoutException</code> describe execution control, not new SMTP statuses.
      Both can contain an optional submission receipt. Before preparation/scheduling or while waiting for a connection, there may be no receipt.
      <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-control-results">Read those receipts before deciding to retry.</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="side"><pre><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withMailSendTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .buildMailer();

MailSend&lt;Void&gt; send = mailer.sendMailAsync(email);

// Called by application code when this notification is obsolete:
send.requestCancellation();

// It resolves after execution exits and required cleanup finishes,
// not merely because cancellation was requested.
send.getCompletion().whenComplete(this::recordCompletion);</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties"># Optional; omitted means no total send deadline.
simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.timeout=PT30S</code></pre>
<p>The same property works in <a href="/spring.html">Spring</a>. The CLI builder option takes ISO-8601 text too:
  <code>--mailer:withMailSendTimeout=PT30S</code>. <code>resetMailSendTimeout()</code> removes a configured total deadline.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>What it bounds</th><th>What it does not mean</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>withSessionTimeout(...)</code></td><td>Individual socket connect/read/write waits.</td><td>Not a budget for the whole send.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withAsyncQueueWaitTimeoutMillis(...)</code></td><td>Caller-side WAIT_FOR_CAPACITY admission.</td><td>Does not cancel already admitted SMTP work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withConnectionPoolClaimTimeoutMillis(...)</code></td><td>Waiting to obtain a pooled transport.</td><td>Not a deadline for using that transport.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withMailSendTimeout(...)</code></td><td>One budget across preparation, admission, pool acquisition, connection/TLS/authentication,
          MIME/security work, SMTP, and required cleanup.</td><td>Not proof of non-acceptance, and not an unconditional return-by time.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The total timeout is disabled by default and must be a positive, nanosecond-representable Duration. Existing shorter socket/acquisition limits
      still apply. A simple batch has one budget for its whole iteration, excluding observer work between emails.
      An open-connection operation gives connection establishment and each sender invocation separate budgets;
      arbitrary application work between sends and final scope cleanup are not charged to a send.</p>
    <p>Supported SJM-owned Angus Sessions capture the socket before connection establishment, including TLS and supported proxy paths.
      Cancellation closes that socket without waiting for Angus's SMTP monitor. A pooled abort invalidates its lease and waits for disposal;
      a retired request cannot reach a later borrower. Queued cancellation removes owned queue work and releases its waiting slot.
      With a caller executor, a retired wrapper cannot send even if the executor cannot remove it.</p>
    <p>A configured total deadline fails before connecting if a selected provider, custom socket factory, CustomMailer, or caller-owned Session
      does not expose supported physical-abort capability. SJM does not silently replace caller-owned Session providers or socket factories.
      Without a total deadline, those integrations keep working, with cancellation at cooperative boundaries only.</p>
    <p>A shared pool cluster used for timed sends needs that capability on every member, because the pool may select any member.
      Once a timed send uses a cluster, newly registered members are checked too, until that cluster has no registered Sessions left.</p>
    <p>DNS, application callbacks, custom data sources, and provider code outside the supported I/O path cannot be forcibly terminated.
      Completion waits for that code and required cleanup to return. Observer work is excluded from the send budget, but the default inline
      observer can still delay completion. Deadline signalling itself never runs observers or CompletableFuture continuations.</p>
    <p>Simple batches stop at the first failed/cancelled/expired attempt. Untouched emails receive no callbacks; a batch cancelled or rejected
      before execution does not open its iterable. Validation, rehearsal, connection tests, and standalone BatchTransportExecutor operations
      are not instrumented by this Mailer deadline or cancellation API.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<a href="#section-batch-and-clustering" id="section-batch-and-clustering" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Connection reuse and clusters</h2>
<section><p class="wide">Queue limits count waiting tasks; pool limits count reusable connections. A worker waiting for a lease is still active.
  Use the direct, simple-batch, open-connection, or pooled path that matches your workload.</p></section>
  <a href="#section-not-reusing-connections" id="section-not-reusing-connections" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 core=true}}How direct connections work{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

  <section>
	<div>
		<p class="wide">
			Direct asynchronous sending schedules send tasks on the mailer's configured <code class="inline language-java">ExecutorService</code>. Each send still opens and closes its own Transport
			connection, managed independently of a connection pool. By default this direct executor is a single-thread executor; provide a custom
			<code class="inline language-java">ExecutorService</code> if you want different concurrency. Every concurrent send still needs its own SMTP connection,
			which is less efficient in high-volume scenarios and can reach the SMTP server's connection limit more quickly.
		</p>
		<div>
			<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder.(..).buildMailer();

mailer.sendMailSync(email); // blocks; failures are thrown here
CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; future = mailer.sendMailAsync(email).getCompletion();

/* Existing default-driven calls remain supported: */
Mailer defaultAsyncMailer = mailerBuilder.(..).async().buildMailer();
defaultAsyncMailer.sendMail(email); // follows the configured async default
</code></pre>
		</div><br/>
		Defining your own thread pool using an ExecutorService (the direct-sending default is
		<code class="inline language-java">Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor</code>):
		<div>
			<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withExecutorService(new MyAwesomeCustomThreadPoolExecutor())</code></pre>
		</div>
		<p class="wide">
			The explicit <code class="inline language-java">Sync</code> and <code class="inline language-java">Async</code> methods ignore the Mailer's
			configured async default. Use <code class="inline language-java">sendMail(email)</code> or the boolean overload only when selecting the mode from
			configuration or runtime data is intentional.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			See <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership</a> for who shuts down the default and custom executors.
		</p>
	</div>
  </section>

  <section>
	<div class="view">
		<p>
			When reconnecting for every message is the only problem you need to solve, use simple sequential batch sending. It sends all provided emails
			over one SMTP connection. It sends one at a time, stops at the first failure and reuses only that connection.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailer.sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails);        // uses the mailer's async default
mailer.sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails, false); // blocks until the batch is done
mailer.sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails, true);  // schedules the whole batch asynchronously
</code></pre>
		<p>
			For sustained high-volume sending, prefer <a href="#section-reusing-connections">pooled connections</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
  </section>

  <section>
	<div class="view">
		<p>
			When the caller owns the queue and needs custom work between successful sends, use
			<code class="inline language-java">withOpenConnection(...)</code>. Simple Java Mail owns and closes the SMTP connection for the callback, while the
			callback decides what to send next. This is useful for database-backed queues where a message should be marked as sent before the next one is read.
		</p>
		<p>
			This form uses the Mailer's built-in SMTP connection. It cannot be combined with a
			<a href="features.html#section-custom-mailer">CustomMailer</a>, which owns its own connection lifecycle. Use
			<code class="inline language-java">sendMail(...)</code> normally and keep any connection reuse inside that implementation.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailer.withOpenConnection(sender -> {
    while (database.hasPendingMail()) {
        Email next = database.nextPendingMail();
        sender.sendMail(next);
        database.markSent(next);
    }
});
</code></pre>
		<p>
			The scoped sender is sequential, not asynchronous, not pooled and not clustered. For asynchronous queueing, higher throughput, pooled connections
			or clustered load balancing, use <a href="#section-reusing-connections">pooled connections</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			Use <code class="inline language-java">sender.sendMailAndGetReceipt(next)</code> instead when the checkpoint needs provider-neutral submission
			status, accepted or unsent recipient groups, and any SMTP response exposed by the provider. A failed or partial submission throws
			<code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionException</code> with the same facts, so mark the queue item only after inspecting that outcome.
		</p>
	</div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-reusing-connections" id="section-reusing-connections" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 batch=true}}Reusing connections with a pool{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

	<section>
		<div>
			<p class="wide">
				Pooled delivery reuses SMTP connections across worker threads. The default setup provides up to four pooled connections,
				which automatically close if inactive for five seconds following their last use. This mechanism is optimal for managing bursts of email traffic, keeping
				up to four connections active as long as needed.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				The connection pool configuration is flexible, allowing adjustments to core connection pool size, maximum pool size, and connection expiry policy.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				This section configures the Simple Java Mail <code>Mailer</code> path. Compare it with the standalone batch facade, direct leases, and
				provider-owned pooling in the <a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html">pool orchestration guide</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Direct sending can use a custom thread pool, while pooled sending also lets those threads reuse Transport connections. This can improve performance even with fewer
				threads, as it eliminates the need to repeatedly open and close connections for each email.
			</p>
		<div>
		<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer pooledMailer = mailerBuilder
	   .(..)
	   .withConnectionPoolCoreSize(2) // keep 2 connections up at all times, automatically refreshed after expiry policy closes it (default 0)
	   .withConnectionPoolMaxSize(10) // scale up to max 10 connections until expiry policy kicks in and cleans up (default 4)
	   .withConnectionPoolClaimTimeoutMillis(TimeUnit.MINUTES.toMillis(1)) // wait max 1 minute for available connection (default about 24.9 days)
	   .withConnectionPoolExpireAfterMillis(TimeUnit.MINUTES.toMillis(30)) // keep connections spinning for half an hour (default 5 seconds)
	   .buildMailer();
	</code></pre>
				<p class="wide">
					Or using properties:
				</p>
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.coresize=2
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.maxsize=10
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.claimtimeout.millis=60000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.expireafter.millis=1800000
	</code></pre>
		  <p class="wide">
			  A live connection pool keeps the JVM running. See <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership</a> for orderly
          application shutdown, including clustered Mailers.
		  </p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>


	<a href="#section-clustering" id="section-clustering" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
	{{#> components/feature-heading-h3 batch=true}}Clustered SMTP load balancing{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

	<section>
		<div>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Use a cluster when several SMTP servers handle the same workload, or when different workloads need their own connection pools.
					Mailers that share a cluster key each register a pool for their configured SMTP server.
				</p>
				<p>
					For each send, the cluster selects one registered pool using its round-robin or random-access strategy.
					This spreads work across the SMTP servers registered for that cluster.
				</p>
				<p>
					For cluster-selected versus exact-Session callbacks outside the full Mailer API, see the
					<a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html#option-batch-facade">standalone batch-module facade</a>.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="side">
				Cluster-level Java config:
				<pre><code>UUID ordersCluster = UUID.fromString("00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301");

Mailer clusteredMailer = mailerBuilder
    .(..) // normal SMTP settings
    .withClusterKey(ordersCluster)
    .withConnectionPoolCoreSize(2)
    .withConnectionPoolMaxSize(10)
    .withConnectionPoolClaimTimeoutMillis(60000)
    .withConnectionPoolExpireAfterMillis(1800000)
    .withConnectionPoolLoadBalancingStrategy(LoadBalancingStrategy.ROUND_ROBIN)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
				or using properties:
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN
# valid values: ROUND_ROBIN, RANDOM_ACCESS</code></pre>
			</div>

			<div class="wide full-width-example">
				<p><strong>Failure behavior in a cluster:</strong></p>
				<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Mailer primary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-primary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
    .buildMailer();
Mailer secondary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-secondary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
    .buildMailer();
Mailer tertiary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-tertiary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
    .buildMailer();

try {
    primary.sendMailSync(email); // one registered pool is selected for this send
} catch (MailException failure) {
    // The selected SMTP submission failed.
    // Its connection is discarded, but its server pool stays in the cluster.
    // Apply your retry, provider-routing, or circuit-breaking policy here.
}</code></pre>
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="wide">
			<strong>Handling a failed send:</strong> Simple Java Mail invalidates the failed transport and reports the failure to your application.
			The server pool remains registered and participates in normal selection for later sends. With synchronous sending, the call throws
			<code class="inline language-java">MailException</code>; with asynchronous sending, the returned
			<a href="features.html#section-handling-async-result"><em>CompletableFuture</em> completes exceptionally</a>.
			Use that failure signal with your application's retry, health-check, circuit-breaking, or provider-routing policy, including its safeguards for
			ambiguous SMTP outcomes.
		</p>

	  <p class="wide">
		  <strong>Note 2:</strong> Connection pool defaults are scoped by cluster key. The first Mailer instance in a cluster sets that cluster's
		  pool defaults; later Mailer instances in the same cluster cannot change them. Different cluster keys can use different pool defaults.
	  </p>

	  <p class="wide">
		  <strong>Note 3:</strong> Using Java API, you can define any number of clusters. Using
		  <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusterkey.uuid</code>, you define one default cluster key.
		  Using <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.*</code>, property files and
		  <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-profiles">Spring</a> can also define
		  separate pool defaults for multiple cluster keys.
	  </p>

		<div class="wide">
			<p><strong>Property-defined cluster configs:</strong></p>
			<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Alias-based config. The alias is only a property-file name; clusterkey.uuid is the actual cluster key.
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.coresize=0
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.maxsize=3
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.claimtimeout.millis=30000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.expireafter.millis=600000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.loadbalancing.strategy=RANDOM_ACCESS

# Direct UUID-keyed config. If clusterkey.uuid is omitted, the alias itself must be a UUID.
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.maxsize=8
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN
</code></pre>
		</div>

		<p class="wide">Choose worker, queue, and connection limits together with your SMTP service's connection and rate limits.
          More connections are useful only while the server and your workload can use them.</p>
	</section>
  <a href="#section-mail-send-observer" id="section-mail-send-observer" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Observing completed mail sends{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Configure one <code class="inline language-java">MailSendObserver</code> when audit, metrics, or progress code should see every completed email
        handled by a reusable Mailer. The builder keeps its usual <code class="inline language-java">with...</code> vocabulary; the
        <code class="inline language-java">onMailSendCompleted(...)</code> method is the callback Simple Java Mail invokes. Configuring another observer
        replaces the previous one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The observer receives one terminal <code class="inline language-java">MailSendOutcome</code> for each reached email. It covers the complete Simple
        Java Mail attempt, including preparation and asynchronous queue time. Its optional <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionReceipt</code>
        is narrower: it contains SMTP submission facts only. Preparation and scheduling failures therefore have no receipt; a
        <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionException</code> outcome contains that exception's exact receipt.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a stream of completed-email progress, not a stream of queued, connecting, leased, or other transport lifecycle events. It does not expose
        the Email, rendered content, connection, pool, proxy, or executor.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer(
        "smtp.example.org", 587,
        "user", "password")
    .withMailSendObserver(
        this::recordMailSendOutcome)
    .buildMailer();

private void recordMailSendOutcome(
        MailSendOutcome outcome) {
    Duration totalTime = Duration.between(
        outcome.getRequestedAt(),
        outcome.getCompletedAt());

    audit.record(
        outcome.getEffectiveMessageId(),
        outcome.isSuccessful(),
        totalTime);

    outcome.getSubmissionReceipt()
        .ifPresent(receipt -> metrics
            .recordSubmissionStatus(
                receipt.getStatus()));

    outcome.getFailure()
        .ifPresent(failure -> metrics
            .recordFailure(failure.getClass()));
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>
        A named observer makes the configuration method and callback roles explicit:
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">final class MetricsMailSendObserver implements MailSendObserver {

    @Override
    public void onMailSendCompleted(MailSendOutcome outcome) {
        Duration sendTime = outcome.getStartedAt()
            .map(startedAt -> Duration.between(
                startedAt, outcome.getCompletedAt()))
            .orElse(Duration.ZERO);

        metrics.recordMailSend(
            outcome.isSuccessful(),
            outcome.isLoggingOnly(),
            sendTime);
    }
}

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withMailSendObserver(new MetricsMailSendObserver())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code class="inline language-java">requestedAt</code> records when the individual attempt begins,
        <code class="inline language-java">readyAt</code> when preparation finishes,
        <code class="inline language-java">startedAt</code> when execution begins, and
        <code class="inline language-java">completedAt</code> when the terminal result exists. Ready-to-start is executor queue time; start-to-completion is
        send execution time. A preparation failure has neither ready nor started time. A scheduling failure has a ready time but no started time.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the default registration, synchronous callbacks run before the call returns and async callbacks before the completion resolves.
        Executor-backed dispatch changes that boundary as described below. Outcomes are frozen before either callback execution or handoff.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code class="inline language-java">isLoggingOnly()</code> reports the Mailer's configured mode, not server acceptance. A successful logging-only
        attempt still has an <code class="inline language-java">UNKNOWN</code> submission receipt because no SMTP server was contacted. Message-ID getters
        return only real initial or effective Message-ID values; missing IDs remain <code class="inline language-java">null</code> rather than receiving a
        synthetic correlation value.
      </p>
      <p>
        The manually runnable
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/MailSendObserverDemoApp.java">MailSendObserverDemoApp</a>
        sends several emails asynchronously and prints each terminal outcome immediately with its Message-ID, queue, send and total time, completion count,
        status, and callback thread. Enable <code class="inline language-java">DemoAppBase</code> logging-only mode to try the stream without an SMTP server.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>



<section>
  <div class="wide">
    <h3>Choose where the observer runs</h3>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Registration</th><th>Before send completion</th><th>Application responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><code>withMailSendObserver(observer)</code></td><td>The callback returns before the synchronous call or async completion.</td>
        <td>Keep it quick and thread-safe; slow work delays that completion and occupies its notifying thread.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withMailSendObserver(observer, executor)</code></td><td>SJM attempts one handoff before completing, without waiting for the callback.</td>
        <td>Own the executor, its queue, rejection policy, and shutdown. Track downstream completion yourself.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>Either overload replaces the whole registration; the one-argument overload restores inline dispatch.
      These are runtime Java integrations, not properties or CLI values, and they are not exposed through OperationalConfig.</p>
    <p>Default callback threads are the calling thread for synchronous sends and preparation/admission failures, the send worker for running async
      work, and a private Mailer completion worker for cancelled/expired work that never started. Different sends can notify concurrently.
      A slow inline callback or completion continuation can delay other terminal notifications on that private worker, but not their deadline signals.</p>
    <p>The executor overload uses the application executor's actual policy. A direct executor or CallerRunsPolicy can still execute inline;
      a blocking execute() can delay handoff. Rejected handoffs are logged as undelivered notifications, without retry or inline fallback.
      A silent-discard executor cannot report a lost notification. Callback RuntimeExceptions are logged and ignored in both modes.</p>
    <p>There are three distinct completions: the send and its cleanup, observer callback/handoff, and whatever downstream processing your observer
      starts. An observer may publish an event to a durable broker for night processing or separately scaled cloud workers. That downstream work
      need not limit SMTP throughput; its persistence, capacity and delivery guarantees belong to that application integration.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wide full-width-example"><pre><code class="language-java">ExecutorService observationWorkers = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withMailSendObserver(outcome -&gt; auditQueue.publish(outcome), observationWorkers)
    .buildMailer();

// Sends may complete before publish() runs. A broker acknowledgement
// is separate again from processing that event on another system.
MailSend&lt;Void&gt; send = mailer.sendMailAsync(email);
send.getCompletion().join();

mailer.close();              // finishes SJM work and handoff attempts
observationWorkers.shutdown(); // application owns this executor
// Await its termination if this application needs all callbacks to finish.
// Await downstream acknowledgements separately if your workflow needs them.</code></pre>
    <p>The manual <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/MailSendExecutionDemoApp.java">MailSendExecutionDemoApp</a>
      requests cancellation and uses an executor-backed observer. It awaits send completion and observer aggregation separately.</p>
  </div>
</section>
  <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle" id="section-mailer-lifecycle" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 batch=true spring=true}}Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div>
      <p class="wide">
        A <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code> is designed to be built once and reused. When your application is finished with it, call
        <code class="inline language-java">mailer.close()</code> directly or let a try-with-resources block call it. Which resources need attention depends
        on how the Mailer is used and whether its executor came from Simple Java Mail or from your application.
      </p>
      <p class="wide">
        Closing a Mailer stops new work on its built-in executor, waits for accepted tasks to finish, and then closes its connection-pool registration.
        It also tracks sends accepted through a caller-owned executor and its private completion worker, without shutting down the caller's executor.
        It waits for observer handoff attempts, not executor-backed callbacks or external downstream processing.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>How the Mailer is used</th>
          <th>What it owns</th>
          <th>What to do at shutdown</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Synchronous direct sending</td>
          <td>Each SMTP connection is closed after the operation. The internal executor starts no worker thread unless an asynchronous operation is scheduled.</td>
          <td>Call <code class="inline language-java">mailer.close()</code> when its application lifetime ends. There are no send futures to await if it was only used synchronously.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Asynchronous sending with the default executor</td>
          <td>The Mailer owns its executor. Direct sending uses a single-thread executor by default.</td>
          <td>Call <code class="inline language-java">mailer.close()</code> to drain accepted work. Still inspect each returned future for send failures.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sending with a custom <code class="inline language-java">ExecutorService</code></td>
          <td>The Mailer owns its SMTP resources; your application owns the executor it supplied.</td>
          <td>Wait for the futures and call <code class="inline language-java">mailer.close()</code>. Shut down the executor separately when its owner is finished with it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Pooled sending</td>
          <td>The Mailer registers its SMTP connection pool and owns its default worker executor.</td>
          <td>Close every Mailer; its accepted sends drain before its pool registration closes. In a cluster, each Mailer has its own pool registration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A Spring-managed Mailer</td>
          <td>The same resources as its underlying configuration.</td>
          <td>Spring calls <code class="inline language-java">close()</code> on the managed Mailer when the application context stops. A Mailer created outside Spring bean management remains your responsibility.</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="wide">
        Use <code class="inline language-java">close()</code> for normal application shutdown. It shuts down an executor created by Simple Java Mail and,
        when pooled delivery is active, closes the connection pool registered for that Mailer. It never shuts down an executor supplied through
        <code class="inline language-java">withExecutorService(...)</code>.
      </p>
      <p class="wide">
        The older <code class="inline language-java">shutdownConnectionPool()</code> method starts the same cleanup and returns a future for draining the
        Mailer-owned work and closing the pool. Repeated calls return the same cleanup future. Despite its name, it also works without a connection pool.
        A successful cleanup does not mean that every send succeeded; those results remain on the individual send futures.
      </p>
      <p class="wide">
        Graceful shutdown does not request cancellation or add a deadline; configured send deadlines still apply. It can wait for slow I/O or an application callback. Call blocking
        <code class="inline language-java">close()</code> from the application's owning thread, not within a send operation or inline observer, which would wait for itself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <h4>Synchronous work</h4>
      <p>
        A try-with-resources block can close the Mailer as soon as the final synchronous call returns.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder
        .buildMailer()) {
    mailer.sendMailSync(email);
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
      <h4>Asynchronous work</h4>
      <p>
        With the built-in executor, try-with-resources drains accepted tasks when leaving the block. Await the futures as well if the caller needs their results.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder
        .async()
        .buildMailer()) {
    CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; first =
            mailer.sendMail(firstEmail).getCompletion();
    CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; second =
            mailer.sendMail(secondEmail).getCompletion();

    CompletableFuture.allOf(first, second)
            .join();
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>
